    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.56028/aetr.16.1.929.2026",
    "abstract": "Located in the southern part of the North China Plain, Zhengzhou has experienced intensified winter temperature fluctuations in recent years due to global climate change. The alternation of cold waves and warm winters has increased the volatility of electricity demand, making trend prediction more challenging. To address this issue, this study develops a winter electricity demand forecasting model for Zhengzhou that incorporates extreme weather scenarios. The model is used to forecast electricity demand in Zhengzhou for January and February 2024. The results show that under normal, warmer-than-usual, and extremely cold scenarios, electricity demand in Zhengzhou increases by 12.5%, 6.6%, and 18.2%, respectively.",
    "field": "Electricity demand forecasting",
    "problem": "Intensified winter temperature fluctuations from alternating cold waves and warm winters increase electricity demand volatility, making conventional forecasting more challenging and less accurate for maintaining a stable power supply.",
    "method": "This study proposes a forecasting model that decomposes electricity demand into baseline and heating-related components, using temperature-based regression under normal, warmer-than-usual, and extremely cold scenarios to predict total winter consumption.",
    "task": "The task is to develop a winter electricity demand forecasting model for Zhengzhou that incorporates extreme weather scenarios to predict consumption under normal, warmer-than-usual, and extremely cold conditions.",
